 "path": ".openclaw/tmp/continual-arc-notes.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC reusable observation\n\nTask family: separator-defined repeated panels. A colored full row/column (often color 3) acts as a divider; the large uniform background is the panel fill. The non-background pattern in a populated panel may be replicated into otherwise empty panels while preserving its within-panel coordinates. In the observed example, a 20x7 grid had two divider rows (rows 6 and 13), creating three 6-row bands. The populated third band (rows 14–19) was copied unchanged into the first two bands, with divider rows preserved. A separate demo showed divider row/column geometry can partition panels and patterns may be reflected across a divider, so compare coordinates and symmetry rather than blindly copying if panels are split both ways.\n"
}
